<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramiro II of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1086">1086</a>–<a href="/years/1157">1157</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Ramiro the Monk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramiro II, called the Monk, was King of Aragon from 1134 to 1137. A Benedictine monk, he was called from the cloister to succeed his brother Alfonso I the Battler, who had died childless. He married to produce an heir, betrothed his infant daughter Petronilla to Ramon Berenguer IV, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, in 1137, and then withdrew to monastic life, creating the union that became the Crown of Aragon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Ramiro II of Aragon" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramiro.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
